Wait, actually, let me reconsider blink target at end step. If I blink Voyager: Voyager ETB → gain 1 life. Is it the second time this turn? Voyager's first ETB was when cast. Blink ETB = second time → draw a card! That's excellent: blink Voyager → gain 1 life AND draw a card, plus Lieutenant gets +1/+1 counter (3/3 trample).\n\
\n\
Hmm wait, \"If this is the second time this ability has resolved this turn, draw a card.\" When Voyager is blinked, it's a new object... does the game remember? The ability resolved once this turn (from the original Voyager). After blink, it's a new permanent/object, so \"this ability has resolved\" count likely resets per object. In MTG rules, the count is per permanent object. A new object would be the first time. So blinking Voyager gives 1 life but no draw. Hmm, but Lieutenant still gets a counter (3/3).\n\
\n\
Blinking Lieutenant: token +1/+1 (3/3), Voyager +1/+1 (3/3). Board: Lieutenant 2/2, Voyager 3/3, token 3/3. \n\
\n\
Blinking Lieutenant seems better for board power: +2 power across team vs +1 power on Lieutenant + 1 life.\n\
\n\
Actually wait — when Lieutenant blinks back, does Ascension add a quest counter? Yes (creature entered). And Lieutenant's ETB pumps. Also does Voyager's ability trigger when Lieutenant enters? Yes — Voyager: \"Whenever this or another Ally you control enters, you gain 1 life.\" Lieutenant is an Ally! So blinking Lieutenant: Voyager triggers (gain 1 life — second time this turn? Voyager's ability resolved once when Voyager itself entered. Lieutenant entering triggers Voyager's ability = second resolution this turn → draw a card!). \n\
\n\
So blinking Lieutenant gives: token +1/+1, Voyager +1/+1, Voyager trigger (gain 1 life + draw a card since second resolution), Ascension quest counter. That's amazing value.\n\
\n\
Wait, let me double check: when I cast Voyager, its ETB triggers: gain 1 life (first resolution). When Lieutenant enters (from blink), Voyager's ability triggers again: gain 1 life, second time → draw a card. Yes!\n\
\n\
But hold on — sequencing during main phase. I cast Lieutenant first (ETB pumps token), then Voyager (ETB gain 1 life; Lieutenant trigger: +1/+1 on Lieutenant). Then end step: Ascension triggers (4 counters), blink Lieutenant → Lieutenant ETB: +1/+1 on token and Voyager (other Allies). Voyager trigger: gain 1 life, 2nd resolution → draw. Ascension: +1 quest counter (5).\n\
\n\
End board: Lieutenant 2/2 trample, Voyager 3/3, token 3/3, plus a drawn card, life at 19. \n\
\n\
Opponent's next turn: they untap with Long Feng 4/5, Raucous Audience 2/1, Swamp 2/2, plus whatever they cast (2 cards in hand + draw). They have Ba Sing Se + Forest + untapped lands: total lands = Ba Sing Se, Foggy Bottom Swamp, Forest, Omashu City, Swamp, Swamp = 6 lands + Raucous Audience mana. They could cast big things. But they have only 2 cards in hand and 22 in library.\n\
\n\
If they attack with Long Feng into my board: I can block with token 3/3 + Voyager 3/3... Long Feng 4/5 trample. Blocking with 3/3: trample 1 through. Or double block: 3/3 + 3/3 = 6 damage, kills Long Feng? Long Feng 4/5 takes 6 → dies. Trample: 4 damage assigned, must assign lethal to first blocker (3), 1 tramples through. They probably won't attack into that, or they trade Long Feng for two of my creatures... Actually double block: they assign 3 to token (lethal), 1 to Voyager. Token dies, Voyager survives (3/3 takes 1). Long Feng takes 6 damage → dies. That's a good trade for me? I lose a 3/3 token, they lose Long Feng. But they have Earthbender Ascension giving counters... eh.\n\
\n\
Alternatively Hakoda play: 3/5 vigilance, blocks Long Feng cleanly each turn, both survive, stalemate. Hakoda also enables casting Allies off the top.\n\
\n\
I think the double-spell play is stronger: more board presence, card draw at end step, more pressure. Opponent at 14; next turn I attack with Lieutenant 2/2, Voyager 3/3, token 3/3 = 8 damage potential, plus Katara Water Tribe's Hope next turn creates another token and pumps.\n\
\n\
Actually, let me also consider: cast Voyager first, then Lieutenant? Lieutenant ETB pumps \"each other Ally\" — if Voyager is already out, Voyager gets +1/+1 too. Order: Voyager first (gain 1 life, first resolution), then Lieutenant: ETB pumps token AND Voyager. Lieutenant's second ability doesn't trigger for itself. Then end step blink Lieutenant: pumps token + Voyager again, Voyager triggers (gain life, 2nd resolution → draw), Ascension counter.\n\
\n\
Board: token 1/1→2/2→3/3, Voyager 2/2→3/3→4/4, Lieutenant 1/1 (no counters on itself! Because when Voyager entered, Lieutenant wasn't out yet). Hmm, Lieutenant stays 1/1. That's worse for Lieutenant but better for Voyager. Total power: 3+4+1 = 8 vs first ordering: Lieutenant 2/2, Voyager 3/3, token 3/3 = 8. Same total. But Lieutenant 1/1 is fragile. Voyager 4/4 is nice.\n\
\n\
Hmm, actually wait: first ordering — Lieutenant first, then Voyager: Voyager ETB triggers Lieutenant's \"another Ally enters\" → Lieutenant gets +1/+1 (2/2). Token gets +1/+1 from Lieutenant ETB (2/2). Voyager 2/2. End step blink Lieutenant: token 3/3, Voyager 3/3, Voyager trigger gain+draw. Total: 2+3+3 = 8 power.\n\
\n\
Second ordering — Voyager first, then Lieutenant: Lieutenant ETB pumps token (2/2) and Voyager (3/3). Blink Lieutenant: token 3/3, Voyager 4/4. Lieutenant 1/1. Total 8 power. Voyager trigger on blink: 2nd resolution → draw.\n\
\n\
Either way I draw a card at end step. The difference: Lieutenant 2/2 + Voyager 3/3 vs Lieutenant 1/1 + Voyager 4/4. A 4/4 Voyager is harder to kill and blocks Long Feng... 4/4 vs 4/5: blocks, both take 4, Voyager dies (4/4 takes 4 lethal), Long Feng survives with 4 damage marked (4/5). Hmm. 3/3 blocking Long Feng: takes 4, dies, Long Feng takes 3.\n\
\n\
Actually for blocking purposes, token 3/3 + Voyager 3/3 double-block kills Long Feng as computed. With Voyager 4/4: block Long Feng with Voyager alone: Long Feng takes 4 (dies? 4/5 takes 4 — no, survives with 4 marked... 5 toughness, 4 damage, survives). Voyager 4/4 takes 4 → dies. Bad. Double block token+Voyager: Long Feng assigns lethal 3 to one, 1 to other; takes 7 damage → dies. Same outcome.\n\
\n\
I'll go with Lieutenant first then Voyager — more even board, Lieutenant 2/2 trample can attack past 2/1s.\n\
\n\
Wait, one more consideration: mana. Casting both uses G,U,W,W → Lieutenant {G}{W}, Voyager {1}{W}: generic 1 can be U. So G,W for Lieutenant; U,W for Voyager. All 4 lands tapped. Fine — opponent's turn, I have no instants anyway.\n\
\n\
Also: should I attack this turn? I already attacked with the two tokens (that's why they're tapped... wait, one token is shown untapped in the decision board? \"Ally Token 1/1 (tapped, token)\" — only p75 listed? The board shows \"Ally Token 1/1 (tapped, token)\" once but earlier there were two tokens p75 and p77. Hmm, in the Decision board summary: bf=[Airbender Ascension (quest=2), Ally Token 1/1 (tapped, token), Forest, Island, North Pole Gates (tapped), Plains, Plains]. Only ONE token? Earlier combat showed two attackers p75 and p77 unblocked. Opponent went 15→14 (took 1?). Hmm, opponent at 14, was 15. So only 1 damage got through? And one token missing... \n\
\n\
Oh wait — maybe Ostrich-Horse blocked one token! Ostrich-Horse was summoning sick but can still block. Ostrich-Horse 3/1 blocks 1/1 token: token dies, Ostrich-Horse takes 1 → dies (1 toughness). That explains Ostrich-Horse in graveyard and one token gone. So combat: one token traded with Ostrich-Horse, other token got through for 1 (15→14). Makes sense.\n\
\n\
OK so I have one 1/1 token (tapped). Recount my board: Ascension (2 counters), 1 token, lands.\n\
\n\
So with double spell: Lieutenant ETB pumps the token to 2/2. End step blink: token 3/3, Voyager 3/3 (if Lieutenant first) — wait let me redo.\n\
\n\
Ordering A (Lieutenant first):\n\
- Cast Lieutenant {G}{W}: ETB → token 1/1→2/2. Ascension 2→3 counters.\n\
- Cast Voyager {1}{W}: ETB → gain 1 (18). Lieutenant trigger → Lieutenant 1/1→2/2. Ascension 3→4.\n\
- End step: Ascension (4 counters) triggers → blink Lieutenant: ETB → token 2/2→3/3, Voyager 2/2→3/3. Voyager trigger → gain 1 (19), 2nd resolution → draw a card. Ascension 4→5.\n\
- Final: Lieutenant 2/2 trample, Voyager 3/3, token 3/3. Life 19. Drew a card.\n\
\n\
Ordering B (Voyager first):\n\
- Cast Voyager: gain 1 (18). Ascension 2→3.\n\
- Cast Lieutenant: ETB → token 2/2, Voyager 3/3. Ascension 3→4.\n\
- End step blink Lieutenant: token 3/3, Voyager 4/4. Voyager trigger: gain 1 (19), 2nd → draw. Ascension 5.\n\
- Final: Lieutenant 1/1, Voyager 4/4, token 3/3. Life 19. Drew a card.\n\
\n\
Both fine. Ordering A gives Lieutenant 2/2 (dies to 2/1 attackers? 2/1 blocks... whatever). Ordering B makes Voyager 4/4 which survives Long Feng combat? No — 4/4 blocking 4/5: Voyager takes 4 → dies. Attacking: 4/4 vs their blockers.\n\
\n\
Hmm, actually one more thought: is Hakoda better? Hakoda 3/5 vigilance + leaves token 1/1. Weaker board, no card draw. I prefer the double spell for tempo and the draw.\n\
\n\
Also consider: could opponent have removal? They used Heartless Act already (in graveyard). 2 cards in hand. They run black — could have more removal. If they kill Lieutenant, my board is Voyager 2/2 + token 1/1. Acceptable risk.\n\
\n\
Decision: Cast Earth King's Lieutenant now, then South Pole Voyager.\n\
\n\
