      "thinking": "I'm in my postcombat main phase. The stack shows Earth King's Lieutenant is on the stack — wait, looking at the log, I said I was casting Earth King's Lieutenant. The stack shows it currently being cast. So I need to... actually the state shows Earth King's Lieutenant on the stack. I have priority. Let me check the details.\n\
\n\
My board:\n\
- Airbender Ascension (2 quest counters)\n\
- Ally Token 1/1 (tapped)\n\
- Forest (tapped)\n\
- Island (untapped)\n\
- North Pole Gates (tapped)\n\
- Plains (tapped)\n\
- Plains (untapped)\n\
\n\
So I have Island + Plains untapped = {U}{W} available, 2 mana.\n\
\n\
Stack: Earth King's Lieutenant {2}{G}{W} presumably — already paid with Forest, North Pole Gates, and one Plains.\n\
\n\
My hand: Hakoda {3}{W}, Katara Water Tribe's Hope {2}{W}{U}{U}, South Pole Voyager {1}{W}.\n\
\n\
Earth King's Lieutenant is on the stack. It will resolve unless opponent responds. Opponent is tapped out (all their lands tapped: Foggy Bottom Swamp tapped, Omashu City tapped, Raucous Audience tapped, Swamps tapped... Ba Sing Se untapped, Forest untapped). Actually they have Ba Sing Se and Forest untapped = {G}{G} potential. They could have a spell but with 2 cards in hand. Heartless Act is in their graveyard already.\n\
\n\
I should pass priority to let Earth King's Lieutenant resolve. When it ETBs, it puts a +1/+1 counter on each other Ally I control (the 1/1 token becomes 2/2), and its ETB triggers Airbender Ascension quest counter (creature entering). That puts Ascension to 3 counters.\n\
\n\
Then with {U}{W} remaining... South Pole Voyager costs {1}{W} — I can't cast it since I have {U}{W}... wait, {1}{W} can be paid with Plains (W) + Island (U) as the generic 1. Yes! {1}{W} = one generic + white. I can pay Island for the 1 and Plains for W. So I can cast South Pole Voyager after Lieutenant resolves.\n\
\n\
South Pole Voyager ETB: it's an Ally entering → triggers Ascension quest counter (to 4), triggers Lieutenant's \"whenever another Ally enters, put +1/+1 counter on Lieutenant\" (Lieutenant becomes +1/+1), and Voyager's own life gain trigger (gain 1 life). That brings Ascension to 4 counters, unlocking the end-step blink.\n\
\n\
At end step, Ascension triggers: exile up to one target creature I control, then return it. Blinking Earth King's Lieutenant would re-trigger its ETB: put +1/+1 counter on each other Ally (token and Voyager), plus its ETB adds another quest counter. Blinking the token does nothing. Blinking Lieutenant is best value — pumps team again.\n\
\n\
Wait, but blinking Lieutenant: when it returns, \"each other Ally creature\" gets counters — token and Voyager each get +1/+1. Also, Lieutenant returns as a new object... it keeps no counters? It returns to the battlefield — counters are removed when it changes zones. So Lieutenant loses its counters but re-pumps others. Hmm, trade-off: Lieutenant would have +1/+1 from Voyager entering (becomes 4/4 base 3/3? Let me check: Earth King's Lieutenant base stats — probably 3/3 or so with trample). If I blink it, it loses accumulated counters but pumps the team. Alternatively blink the Ally token which has counters — no, tokens cease to exist when exiled! Blinking a token = token is gone forever. So blink Lieutenant or Voyager.\n\
\n\
Blinking Voyager: Voyager ETB again → gain 1 life (second time this turn? The ability says \"If this is the second time this ability has resolved this turn, draw a card\"). If Voyager's ability resolved once already this turn, the blinked Voyager's trigger would be the second resolution → draw a card! Also Voyager entering triggers Lieutenant's counter ability and Ascension quest counter. That's nice value.\n\
\n\
Blinking Lieutenant: pumps team but loses Lieutenant's counters.\n\
\n\
Which is better? Let's think: after Lieutenant resolves and Voyager enters:\n\
- Token: 1/1 +1/+1 counter = 2/2\n\
- Lieutenant: base (let me check oracle text - I'll assume 3/3 trample) + 1 counter from Voyager = 4/4\n\
- Voyager: 2/2\n\
\n\
End step blink options:\n\
a) Blink Voyager: Voyager ETB → gain 1 life, draw a card (second resolution). Lieutenant gets another counter (5/5). Ascension gets 5th counter. Voyager returns as 2/2.\n\
b) Blink Lieutenant: token gets another counter (3/3), Voyager gets a counter (3/3), Lieutenant returns as base 3/3. Ascension gets 5th counter.\n\
\n\
Option (a) draws a card and gains life, keeps Lieutenant big. Option (b) pumps team. I think (a) is better — card draw is valuable, and Lieutenant stays 5/5.\n\
\n\
Actually wait — when Voyager returns, is it \"the second time this ability has resolved this turn\"? The ability says \"Whenever this or another Ally you control enters, you gain 1 life. If this is the second time this ability has resolved this turn, draw a card.\" The first ETB was the first resolution. The blink ETB is the second resolution → draw a card. But also, does the Voyager's ability trigger from its own ETB? \"Whenever {this} or another Ally you control enters\" — yes, it triggers on its own ETB.\n\
\n\
Also note: when Voyager is exiled and returns, it's a new object, but the \"second time this ability has resolved this turn\" refers to the ability resolving — it counts resolutions of that ability from that permanent? Actually for a new object, its ability hasn't resolved before... Hmm, rules-wise: \"If this is the second time this ability has resolved this turn\" — the game tracks how many times the ability of that object resolved. A new object would be the first time for the new object. Actually, I believe the rule is that the triggered ability's resolution count is tracked per object. Since the blinked Voyager is a new object, its ability resolving would be the first time for that object, so no card draw. Hmm, that's a nuance.\n\
\n\
Let me not overthink. The simple plan: let Lieutenant resolve, cast Voyager, then at end step choose the blink target. Probably Lieutenant for team pump, or Voyager. Let me decide when I get there.\n\
\n\
Actually, let's reconsider mana: I have Island + Plains untapped. South Pole Voyager {1}{W}: tap Island for the generic, Plains for W. Good.\n\
\n\
Right now the stack has Earth King's Lieutenant — I cast it and presumably paid. I have priority with it on the stack. I should pass priority to let it resolve (opponent may respond, but they're mostly tapped). The instruction says \"Continue playing. Call pass_priority.\"\n\
\n\
So call pass_priority now.",
