      "reasoning": "The end-step blink from Airbender Ascension is live. Best target is South Pole Voyager: its ETB life-gain already resolved once this turn, so blinking it triggers it a second time — which draws a card — plus it pumps Earth King's Lieutenant and adds another quest counter. Blinking the token would just kill it, and blinking Lieutenant only pumps without the card draw.",
      "thinking": "The Airbender Ascension end-step trigger is on the stack. I need to select up to one creature to blink. Options: Ally Token (would die if exiled - tokens cease to exist), Earth King's Lieutenant (ETB pumps other Allies again — my token and Voyager would get +1/+1 counters), South Pole Voyager (ETB gains 1 life; second Ally ETB trigger of the turn... wait, South Pole Voyager's life gain already resolved once this turn when it entered. If I blink Voyager, its ETB triggers again — \"If this is the second time this ability has resolved this turn, draw a card.\" So blinking Voyager would gain 1 life AND draw a card!).\n\
\n\
Wait, but also Earth King's Lieutenant's ETB trigger: when Lieutenant enters, put a +1/+1 counter on each other Ally. Also Lieutenant's second ability: whenever another Ally enters, put a counter on Lieutenant. If I blink Lieutenant, the token and Voyager each get a +1/+1 counter.\n\
\n\
Blinking Voyager: Voyager ETB — gain 1 life, and since it's the second time this ability resolved this turn, draw a card. Also Lieutenant's \"whenever another Ally enters\" triggers — Lieutenant gets a +1/+1 counter. Also Ascension gets a quest counter (5).\n\
\n\
That's clearly better: blink South Pole Voyager to draw a card, gain 1 life, and pump Lieutenant.\n\
\n\
Note \"up to one\" — I could choose no target, but blinking Voyager is great value. Choose p84.",
