Also note: I should attack FIRST and then waterbend? No — waterbend is \"activate only during your turn\", can be activated anytime on my turn. If I activate before declaring attackers, they know. If I activate after blockers are declared... waterbend is an activated ability, can I activate it during combat? \"Activate only during your turn\" — yes, any time during my turn including combat. So I could declare attackers, let them declare blockers, THEN waterbend? Wait, but if I waterbend after blockers, the damage math is locked into their bad blocks. But they might block differently if they know waterbend is coming. Since they have no hand, they might not expect it... but a competent opponent sees Katara's ability. Actually, if I declare attackers without waterbend, they might not block at all (taking 9, going to 1) to preserve their board! Then I waterbend and... they've already not blocked, I deal 5×6=30. Wait no — if they don't block, damage is 9 (without waterbend) → they go to 1. Then waterbend in postcombat? Damage already dealt. Hmm.\n\
\n\
Sequence: declare attackers → they declare blockers → if they didn't block, activating waterbend after blockers but before damage makes unblocked creatures 6/6 → 30 damage. If they do block 3, two through → waterbend → 12 damage. Either way lethal! \n\
\n\
So: declare attackers with all 5, see blocks, then activate waterbend X=5 or 6 before damage. That's the optimal sequence. If they block 3 creatures, waterbend for lethal. If they don't block, waterbend for lethal too. \n\
\n\
But careful: I need mana open. I have 5 untapped lands. Don't cast anything precombat. Should I play my land drop? Playing Meditation Pools (tapped) gives future mana; playing Island/Plains gives +1 mana now. If I play a land, that's fine — but it telegraphs nothing. Actually, play the land now: more mana = bigger X. Play Plains (basic, untapped). Then 6 mana available.\n\
\n\
Wait, but should I worry that playing a land triggers... no, my Ascension triggers on creature ETB, not landfall. Fine.\n\
\n\
Hmm, one more consideration: Hakoda. Should I cast Hakoda precombat for a bigger attack? Hakoda 3/5 vigilance Ally: ETB triggers Voyager (gain 1 life — first resolution this turn? Voyager's ability: \"If this is the second time this ability has resolved this turn, draw a card.\" Each turn resets. So first trigger: gain 1 life only), Ascension gets 10th counter. Then attack with 6 creatures: 3/3, 3/5, 2/2, 2/2, 1/1, 1/1 vs 3 blockers → 3 through. Without waterbend: if they block 3 biggest, three 1/1s... wait 6 attackers, 3 blockers, 3 through. Minimum: they block 3/5, 3/3, 2/2 → through: 2/2, 1/1, 1/1 = 4 damage. Then waterbend X=2 (4 spent on Hakoda, 2 left): all 2/2 → through 3×2=6 damage. Total mana 6. Hmm, or cast Hakoda and don't waterbend: 4 damage through. Worse than straight waterbend X=6 (12 through). \n\
\n\
Straight waterbend X=6 with 5 attackers: 3 blocked, 2 through = 12 damage. Lethal. That's the play.\n\
\n\
Actually, even better: declare attackers first. Their blocks: they must block to survive? Without waterbend, 9 power attacking vs 10 life — they can take 9 and go to 1, keeping all blockers. If they take it, I waterbend X=6 after blocks (no blocks) → 30 damage, dead. If they block 3, waterbend → 12 through, dead. There's no good option for them. Unless they use Foggy Bottom to draw into something... even so.\n\
\n\
One risk: they activate Ba Sing Se earthbend? Sorcery only, can't during combat. Earthbender Ascension landfall pump — needs land ETB, sorcery speed for them on my turn? No.\n\
\n\
OK here's my sequence:\n\
1. Play Plains (land drop) — 6 mana.\n\
2. Go to combat, declare all 5 attackers.\n\
3. After blockers, activate Katara's waterbend X=6.\n\
4. Lethal.\n\
\n\
Actually wait — should I activate waterbend precombat to guarantee? No benefit; better to wait for blocks. But careful: the choice list right now shows \"Katara, Water Tribe's Hope [id=p7, activate]\" available in precombat main. I should pass to combat first. But if I pass precombat main with \"choice=no\", does it skip to combat or end turn? Passing moves to next phase (begin combat). Good.\n\
\n\
Also should I play the land? Yes — Plains, untapped, 6th mana. Doesn't telegraph anything meaningful.\n\
\n\
Actually, hmm, one thing: if I play Plains and pass, opponent might suspect. Whatever — no hand, no interaction except drawing with Foggy Bottom (needs 4 mana: Foggy Bottom + Omashu + 2 Swamps = exactly 4... but Swamps are creatures/lands that can tap for B; tapping them for mana means they can't block! If they tap Swamps for mana to draw, fewer blockers → more lethal. Fine.)\n\
\n\
Also the Swamp land-creatures: if they tap for mana, they can't block. OK.\n\
\n\
Let me also double-check Katara's waterbend cost: \"Waterbend {X}: Creatures you control have base power and toughness X/X until end of turn. X can't be 0.\" The cost is {X} — I choose X and pay X generic mana. With 6 mana, X=6. \n\
\n\
Also, does \"base power and toughness X/X\" override counters? No — counters apply after setting base. The 2/2 token (1 counter) becomes 6/6+1 = 7/6. Doesn't matter for lethal.\n\
\n\
Plan confirmed. First action: play Plains. Then pass to combat.\n\
\n\
Actually, let me reconsider whether to attack with ALL creatures including South Pole Voyager. Yes — need 5 attackers so 2 get through. All in.\n\
\n\
Also, any reason to hold back for defense? If I kill them this turn, no. If somehow they survive (they draw a fog?), my creatures are 6/6 until end of turn but revert after; my board is still strong with the blink engine. Go for lethal.\n\
\n\
Let me also reply to chat — opponent has been sending repeated messages about Katara. I should chat at least once. Something like \"Katara's about to end it — waterbend for lethal!\" Keep it friendly.\n\
\n\
First: play Plains.",
